Police investigating the Don Toliver concert evacuation in Manchester believe a “small group of organised criminals” were behind a series of robberies at the show.

More than 12,000 people were evacuated from the Co-op Live Arena on Sunday (November 22) after an unknown substance was released into the crowd, leading police to declare a major incident shortly after 10pm.

### 🎤 Key Highlights & Community Reactions

One woman was taken to Manchester Royal Infirmary, while a further 88 people underwent decontamination at the scene before being taken to the neighbouring Etihad Stadium.

Earlier this week, Greater Manchester Police (GMP) revealed that they had received nine reports of jewellery being stolen at the concert and were investigating a potential link between the thefts and the release of the substance .

In a new update issued yesterday (November 27), Assistant Chief Constable Tom Harding said officers suspected the group had “targeted people in the crowd to commit a series of robberies on the night”.

Detectives are reviewing CCTV and mobile phone footage and are seeking to contact more than 100 people who attended the concert to assist with their enquiries. No arrests have been made, and the substance has yet to be identified.

